diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — complete loss of inter-campus connectivity — is not observable in the live network. Phase 1 reachability tested 28 host pairs spanning both campus segments (the mswitch0 cluster serving 172.16.10/20/30.0/24 on pc5–pc14, and the sshswitch cluster serving 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24 on pc1–pc4 and dc_pc5); every cross-campus pair returned 0% packet loss. Phase A infrastructure sweep flagged zero devices: all 15 end-hosts have correctly assigned IPs and default routes, no ACL drop fingerprints, no static ARP anomalies, no gateway divergence. The only l2_snapshot flags were null-MAC tunnel interfaces (tunl0/gre0, all DOWN) — a normal Linux kernel artefact, not a conflict. Phase B OSPF is fully healthy: 9/9 FRR daemons operational, all adjacencies Full, and the correct 17 routes installed in every router's FIB; the inter-campus forwarding chain (router16 → router7 → router8 → router17, with redundant paths via router10 and router11/router1) carries live traffic correctly. Phase D shows no resource pressure on any device and no DNS misconfiguration (all hosts consistently have no nameserver, ruling out a resolver-driven reachability illusion). No flap script, carrier-counter spike, or routing instability trace exists to explain a past intermittent event. Root cause: no fault is present. The ticket describes a transient or incorrectly diagnosed condition; the network fabric is operating normally. Fix: no corrective action is required; if the issue recurs, capture syslog and OSPF neighbor state at the moment of failure to identify any transient adjacency drop.
=== END ===